<crash-analysis>
The crash is caused by a stack-buffer-overflow in the `prvTidystrrep` function in `tmbstr.c`. This function is called by `tidyMessageCreateInitV` to replace placeholders in error messages.

The `prvTidystrrep` function uses a fixed-size local buffer `buf` of 1024 bytes to construct the new string. When a long string is passed as an attribute in the HTML input, the error message formatting in `formatAttributeReport` can generate a very long string. This long string is then passed to `prvTidystrrep` through a series of function calls, eventually causing a buffer overflow when `strcat` is used to build the final message in `prvTidystrrep`.

The immediate cause of the crash is the use of a fixed-size buffer in `prvTidystrrep`. This function should be modified to use dynamic memory allocation to handle strings of arbitrary length. For example, it could use `malloc` and `realloc` to allocate a buffer of the correct size.

A possible patch would be to calculate the required buffer size before performing any string operations and then allocate a buffer of that size dynamically.

```c
// In prvTidystrrep, tmbstr.c

// OLD CODE
// {
//     char buf[1024];
//     ...
//     strcpy(buf, ...);
//     strcat(buf, ...);
//     ...
// }

// NEW CODE
{
char *p = strstr(buffer, str);
if (p)
{
size_t len_rep = strlen(rep);
size_t len_buffer = strlen(buffer);
size_t len_str = strlen(str);
size_t new_len = len_buffer - len_str + len_rep;
char *new_buffer = (char *)malloc(new_len + 1);
if (new_buffer)
{
char *dest = new_buffer;
// Copy the part before the match
memcpy(dest, buffer, p - buffer);
dest += p - buffer;
// Copy the replacement
memcpy(dest, rep, len_rep);
dest += len_rep;
// Copy the part after the match
strcpy(dest, p + len_str);
// Copy the new string back to the original buffer
// Note: This assumes 'buffer' is large enough. If not, the function
// signature and its usage would need to be changed to handle
// re-allocation of the buffer itself.
if (new_len < 1024) // A safe-guard, assuming original buffer size logic
{
strcpy(buffer, new_buffer);
}
free(new_buffer);
}
}
}
```
The above is a conceptual fix. The actual implementation would need to consider the memory management of `buffer` itself, which is managed outside of this function. A better fix would be to change the function to return a new dynamically allocated string rather than modifying the buffer in place.
</crash-analysis>

